    "created_at": "2026-11-12T15:25:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-12T15:25:53Z",
    "body": "Addition from the maintainer's side: add a **hat processing** recipe (the \"hating\" operation in the original request). This is a two-setup method where five faces are finished in the first setup and the part is flipped once for the sixth:\n\n1. **Setup 1, five faces:** start from oversize stock. Hold the stock by its bottom, either a dovetail or a reduced \"hat\" step machined beforehand, or extra stock left in the vise jaws. Machine the top and all four sides to final size, leaving the extra stock on the bottom so the finished part sits on a wider brim (the \"hat\").\n2. **Setup 2, flip:** turn the part over, hold it on the finished sides (soft jaws, or a fixture), carry stock from setup 1, set the WCS on the finished part, then face and profile off the hat to final thickness.\n\nThe recipe should cover:\n- how to size the stock and the hat (height left for grip, and clearance so the tool reaches the sides' full depth);\n- a stock that carries from setup 1 to setup 2;\n- the WCS in each setup;\n- soft jaws or a fixture for setup 2 (link the workholding section, #1262);\n- what to check in the simulation: the tool stays clear of the vise jaws in setup 1, and setup 2 removes only the hat.\n\nIt sits naturally beside \"square up a block in two setups\", as the faster alternative when the part's sides can all be finished in setup 1.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
